from core import L
L(9,'layers-switching','Layering, switching and network performance','Principles of Layering;Circuit switching;Packet switching;Virtual circuit;Performance metrics','Basic units and rates.',
'A network separates services into layers so each solves a manageable part of delivery. Packets share infrastructure, while circuits reserve capacity. Delay depends on several physical and queueing effects, so bandwidth and latency are not interchangeable.',
'''Layers and encapsulation::An application message becomes transport data, then a network packet, then a link frame under common Internet layering. Each layer adds addressing/control information appropriate to its scope. A router forwards network-layer packets; a typical Ethernet switch forwards link-layer frames. End-to-end services and per-link services solve different problems.
||Switching models::Circuit switching reserves a route or channel allocation, potentially with setup overhead and unused reserved capacity. Packet switching shares links among packets and can queue under load. Datagram packets can be routed independently; a virtual circuit establishes logical forwarding state before transfer, typically using a label on subsequent packets. A logical virtual circuit is not necessarily a physically reserved circuit.
||Delay and throughput::Transmission delay=L/R for L bits on rate R; propagation delay=distance/signal speed. Processing and queueing add further delay. Store-and-forward requires a full packet before forwarding in the basic model. Bottleneck link rate constrains sustained throughput. With multiple packets and links, serialization can be pipelined; don’t multiply entire end-to-end latency by packet count automatically.''',
'''Send a 1500-byte packet over 12 Mbps::Convert to bits: 1500×8=12000.~Transmission delay=12000/(12×10⁶)=0.001 s.~That is 1 ms, excluding propagation and queueing.
||Two store-and-forward links at the same rate, no other delay::The first packet needs one transmission time on each link, total 2t.~For N packets, after the first link finishes one it can send the next while the next link forwards the previous.~Completion is (N+1)t for two equal links under these assumptions, not 2Nt.''',
'Convert bytes to bits before dividing by bit rate.||Faster bandwidth does not reduce propagation distance.',
'Transmission=L/R; propagation=d/v.||Bottleneck capacity limits throughput.',
[('A 1000-byte packet uses an 8 Mbps link. What is transmission delay in milliseconds?',1,'Convert bytes to bits.','8000/(8×10⁶)=0.001 s=1 ms.'),('Propagation distance is 600 km and speed 200000 km/s. What is propagation delay in milliseconds?',3,'Divide distance by speed and convert seconds.','600/200000=0.003 s=3 ms.')])
L(9,'link-layer','Error detection, MAC and Ethernet','Data link layer;Error detection;Medium Access Control;Ethernet;CRC','Binary polynomials, frames and shared media.',
'The link layer moves frames over one link and manages its local addressing and error checks. Error detection identifies some corrupt frames; it is not automatically error correction. Shared-medium access rules matter only where senders actually contend for the same medium.',
'''Parity, checksums and CRC::A parity bit detects any odd number of bit flips but can miss an even number. Internet-style checksums use one’s-complement arithmetic. CRC treats bits as polynomial coefficients over GF(2): addition/subtraction is XOR. Append r zeros for a degree-r generator, divide, and append the r-bit remainder. A receiver divides the received codeword; a nonzero remainder detects an error.
||Medium access::ALOHA transmits with probabilistic contention; carrier-sense protocols listen before transmitting. Classic half-duplex Ethernet uses CSMA/CD, detecting collisions and backing off. The minimum frame time must support collision detection over the collision domain under its physical assumptions. Full-duplex switched Ethernet has separate bidirectional links and does not use collision detection.
||Ethernet switching::MAC addresses identify local link interfaces. A learning switch records the source MAC and ingress port. Known destination addresses are forwarded to the corresponding port; unknown destinations and relevant broadcasts are flooded according to VLAN/topology rules. Routers separate network-layer domains; a link switch does not usually choose routes by IP prefix.''',
'''Detect a single flipped bit with even parity::Suppose data has an even number of 1s and parity bit 0.~One flipped bit changes overall parity to odd.~The receiver detects mismatch, but cannot locate the bad bit from parity alone.
||CRC using data 1001 and generator 1011::Generator degree is 3, so divide appended data 1001000 by 1011 using XOR.~First XOR gives 0010000; align again and XOR to get 0000110.~Remainder is 110; transmit 1001110. Dividing this codeword by 1011 gives zero remainder.''',
'CRC division uses XOR without carries.||Modern full-duplex Ethernet does not use CSMA/CD.',
'Degree-r CRC appends r check bits.||Switch learns source MAC, forwards by destination MAC.',
[('A degree-4 CRC generator adds how many remainder bits?',4,'The remainder has at most degree r−1.','Four check bits are appended.'),('An even-parity codeword suffers exactly one bit flip. What is the number of parity violations detected?',1,'One flip changes parity.','The single parity check fails once; it detects but does not locate the error.')])
L(9,'routing','Distance-vector and link-state routing','Distance vector routing;Link state routing;Count to infinity','Graphs, shortest paths and link costs.',
'Routers need a distributed way to learn where to send packets. Distance-vector exchanges route estimates with neighbours; link-state distributes topology so each router can compute routes locally. The information each approach shares explains its failure modes.',
'''Distance-vector update::A router x estimates Dx(y)=minᵥ[c(x,v)+Dv(y)] over neighbours v. Each advertisement supplies a neighbour’s current estimate. Updates may be asynchronous and transient inconsistencies arise when links fail. A destination that becomes unreachable may have increasing estimates passed around, producing count-to-infinity.
||Mitigations and link-state::Split horizon avoids advertising a route back on the interface from which it was learned; poisoned reverse explicitly advertises an infinite metric in that direction. These do not solve every possible loop topology. Link-state routers discover neighbours/costs, flood sequenced link-state advertisements and run Dijkstra on their assembled topology. Sequence numbers and aging prevent stale information from circulating indefinitely.
||Forwarding versus routing::Routing computes and updates the control-plane table; forwarding uses it for individual packets. A next hop need not be the destination itself. Correct convergence is distinct from instantaneous consistency during updates. Compare control traffic, stored knowledge and recovery behaviour rather than assuming one protocol is universally superior.''',
'''Update destination D using two neighbours::Neighbour A costs 2 and advertises distance 5; candidate cost is 7.~Neighbour B costs 4 and advertises distance 1; candidate cost is 5.~Choose B with total 5. The direct link price alone did not determine the best route.
||Understand a stale estimate after failure::A previously reaches D directly, while B’s route to D uses A.~After A loses the destination link, B may still advertise its old route through A.~If A trusts that advertisement, A and B can route toward each other while increasing metrics; stale dependencies create the loop.''',
'Distance-vector advertisements are distances, not full topology maps in the basic model.||Split horizon is not a universal cure for every multi-router loop.',
'Dx(y)=min neighbour(link cost+neighbour distance).||Link-state computes shortest paths from flooded topology.',
[('Link to a neighbour costs 3 and its advertised destination distance is 7. What candidate total is offered?',10,'Add local and advertised costs.','3+7=10.'),('Candidates are 8,12 and 6. What destination metric is selected?',6,'Take the smallest feasible candidate.','min(8,12,6)=6.')])
L(9,'ipv4','IPv4, CIDR and network address translation','IPv4;CIDR Notation;Network Address Translation;Longest prefix','Binary addresses and subnet masks.',
'An IPv4 address contains 32 bits. CIDR says how many leading bits define the routing prefix. NAT rewrites selected addresses and ports, often allowing several private hosts to share a public address, but it changes end-to-end addressing assumptions.',
'''CIDR arithmetic::A /p prefix leaves 32−p varying bits and contains 2^(32−p) total addresses. In conventional multiaccess subnets, network and broadcast addresses are not assignable hosts, giving two fewer; /31 point-to-point and /32 cases require different treatment. Masking an address with the subnet mask gives its network prefix. Block boundaries follow the mask, not arbitrary decimal rounding.
||Longest-prefix match::A packet destination may match several routes. Select the matching route with the largest prefix length, because it identifies the most specific region. The default /0 matches everything but loses to any more-specific match. A route’s prefix need not match a classful address boundary; CIDR replaced that restriction.
||NAT/PAT::Address translation rewrites private/public addresses; port translation distinguishes multiple internal transport sessions sharing an external IP. The device keeps mappings and updates required checksums. Incoming connections need a mapping or explicit forwarding arrangement. NAT is not equivalent to a complete security policy and can complicate protocols embedding addresses in payloads.''',
'''Find the /26 network containing 192.168.1.70::There are 6 host bits, so blocks contain 64 addresses.~The last-octet ranges begin 0,64,128,192; 70 lies in 64–127.~Network is 192.168.1.64, conventional broadcast .127, with hosts .65–.126.
||Choose among /0,/16,/24 matching a destination::All three cover the destination in this example.~The /24 route has the longest prefix.~Forward using its next hop; the fact that /16 covers more addresses does not give it priority.''',
'Total addresses and assignable hosts are different counts.||Do not subtract two blindly for /31 or /32.',
'Addresses=2^(32−p).||Choose the longest matching route prefix.',
[('How many total IPv4 addresses are in a /27 prefix?',32,'There are 5 host bits.','2⁵=32 addresses.'),('How many conventionally assignable host addresses are in a /26 multiaccess subnet?',62,'Subtract network and broadcast from the total.','64−2=62 hosts under the stated convention.')])
L(9,'fragmentation','IPv4 fragmentation step by step','IPv4 Fragmentation;MTU;Fragment offsets','IPv4 headers, byte counts and division.',
'An IP datagram may exceed a link’s maximum transmission unit. Fragmentation divides its payload while preserving enough information to reassemble at the destination. Headers are repeated for every fragment, and offsets locate payload bytes rather than whole datagram bytes.',
'''Payload capacity::MTU limits the total IP fragment length including its own IP header. With header H, payload capacity is MTU−H. Every nonfinal fragment payload must be a multiple of 8 bytes, so use floor((MTU−H)/8)×8 when creating maximal nonfinal fragments. The final payload may be smaller and need not be an eight-byte multiple.
||Header interpretation::Fragments share the relevant source,destination,protocol and identification association. The fragment offset measures where this payload starts in the original payload, in units of 8 bytes. MF=1 says more fragments follow; MF=0 identifies the final portion, not necessarily a complete unfragmented original. DF forbids fragmentation, so an oversize packet must be handled by failure/feedback under the protocol instead.
||Reassembly reasoning::Add payload lengths to verify the original payload total. Calculate offsets cumulatively from payload, excluding repeated headers. IPv4 permits routers to fragment when allowed; destination reassembly handles the pieces. Loss of one needed fragment prevents normal complete delivery of the original datagram, adding a reason large fragmented packets can be fragile.''',
'''Fragment a 4000-byte datagram with 20-byte header across MTU 1500::Original payload=4000−20=3980; nonfinal payload cap=1480, already divisible by 8.~Payload pieces are 1480,1480,1020; total lengths are 1500,1500,1040.~Offsets are 0,1480/8=185,(1480+1480)/8=370; MF values are 1,1,0.
||Fragment a 1020-byte datagram with header 20 across MTU 620::Original payload=1000; maximum nonfinal payload=600.~Pieces are 600 and 400 bytes.~Offsets are 0 and 75; total fragment lengths are 620 and 420.''',
'Offset units are 8 bytes, not bytes.||Subtract the original header before splitting, then add a header to each fragment.',
'Nonfinal payload=floor((MTU−H)/8)×8.||Offset=cumulative prior payload/8.',
[('A fragment follows 1200 bytes of original payload. What is its fragment offset field?',150,'Offsets use eight-byte units.','1200/8=150.'),('An IP datagram is 2020 bytes with 20-byte header. What is its payload size in bytes?',2000,'Remove the header.','2020−20=2000 payload bytes.')])
L(9,'tcp-web','TCP flow/congestion control, sockets, DNS and HTTP','TCP flow control;TCP congestion control;Socket API;DNS;HTTP','Layering, packet loss and rates.',
'TCP transports an ordered byte stream using sequence numbers, acknowledgments and retransmission. The receiver’s capacity and the network’s capacity are separate limits. Application protocols then interpret those delivered bytes or use other transport mechanisms.',
'''Reliability and windows::TCP sequence numbers refer to bytes, and cumulative acknowledgments identify the next expected byte under the usual model. A receive window rwnd advertises buffering capacity. A congestion window cwnd estimates what the sender should inject into the network. The usable limit is approximately min(rwnd,cwnd) minus bytes already outstanding, subject to further implementation rules.
||Congestion control::In the classical teaching model, slow start grows cwnd roughly exponentially per RTT until a threshold or loss indication. Congestion avoidance grows more slowly, often about one MSS per RTT. Timeout and duplicate-ACK loss reactions differ across variants; read the specified algorithm. Bandwidth-delay product estimates data needed in flight to keep a path busy; a too-small window limits throughput.
||Sockets, DNS and HTTP::A TCP server commonly socket/bind/listen/accepts; a client connects, then both send/receive a byte stream without preserved message boundaries. DNS maps names and other records through hierarchy and caches, commonly using UDP but also TCP. HTTP defines methods, status codes, headers and message semantics. Persistent connections reuse transport. HTTP/3 runs over QUIC/UDP, so do not assume every HTTP version uses TCP.''',
'''Compute sending allowance::rwnd=16000 bytes, cwnd=10000 bytes and outstanding=4000.~Limit=min(16000,10000)=10000.~Additional permissible in-flight data is 10000−4000=6000 bytes in the simplified model.
||Sequence and ACK numbers::A segment starts at sequence 100 and carries 200 bytes.~Those byte numbers are 100 through 299.~If contiguous delivery succeeds, the cumulative ACK is 300, the next expected byte, not 299.''',
'Flow control protects the receiver; congestion control protects the network.||A TCP read need not correspond to one send call.',
'Usable allowance=min(rwnd,cwnd)−outstanding.||ACK identifies next expected byte.',
[('rwnd=12000,cwnd=8000,outstanding=3000 bytes. What is additional allowance?',5000,'Apply the minimum before subtracting.','min(12000,8000)−3000=5000.'),('A contiguous segment starts at sequence 500 and carries 100 bytes. What ACK is expected?',600,'ACK the byte after the last delivered byte.','Bytes 500–599 are delivered, so ACK=600.')])
L(10,'quantitative','Quantitative aptitude: ratios, percentages and work','Quantitative aptitude;Percentages;Ratios;Work rates','Arithmetic and simple algebra.',
'Quantitative aptitude usually rewards a clean model rather than lengthy computation. Define units and the reference quantity first. A percentage is relative to a base; a work time converts to a rate before people working together can be combined.',
'''Ratios and averages::If A:B=a:b, write A=ak and B=bk. This preserves the relationship while totals determine k. A weighted average is total weighted value divided by total weight, not the unweighted average of subgroup averages. Check whether group sizes are equal before averaging percentages.
||Successive changes::Increase by p% multiplies by 1+p/100; decrease by q% multiplies by 1−q/100. Combine multipliers to find overall change. Equal percentage rises and falls do not cancel because their bases differ. Profit percentage uses cost price as its base unless explicitly redefined.
||Work and speed::A worker completing one job in t days works at 1/t jobs per day under constant-rate assumptions. Independent rates add for shared work; time is reciprocal total rate. Distance=rate×time with consistent units. Relative speed adds for opposite travel directions and subtracts for same-direction catch-up.''',
'''Apply +20%, then −20% to 100::After the rise, value=120.~The reduction is 20% of 120, which is 24.~Final value=96, a 4% overall decrease.
||A finishes in 6 days and B in 3 days::Rates are 1/6 and 1/3 jobs/day.~Combined rate=1/2 job/day.~Time for one job=1/(1/2)=2 days under constant independent rates.''',
'Percentages of different bases cannot be simply added.||Add work rates, not completion times.',
'Successive changes multiply.||Weighted mean=weighted total/total weight.',
[('A price of 200 rises by 15%. What is its new price?',230,'Multiply by 1.15.','200×1.15=230.'),('Two workers each finish a job in 8 days. Together how many days do they need?',4,'Add their equal rates.','1/8+1/8=1/4 job/day, so 4 days.')])
L(10,'verbal-reasoning','Verbal, analytical and spatial aptitude','Verbal aptitude;Analytical aptitude;Spatial aptitude','Reading comprehension and careful constraint tracking.',
'Reasoning tasks ask you to respect exactly the available evidence. In verbal problems, grammatical role and meaning constrain the answer; in arrangements, each clue reduces the allowed states. Spatial questions require tracking invariants through transformations rather than guessing from familiarity.',
'''Language and comprehension::For grammar, identify subject, verb, modifiers and required agreement. Distinguish possession (their), contraction (they’re) and location (there). For passages, separate explicit statements from reasonable but unsupported assumptions. An inference must follow from the passage, not merely be plausible in real life. Analogy questions should preserve the exact relationship, such as increasing intensity or part-to-whole.
||Analytical constraints::Translate clues into explicit relations: before, immediately before, adjacent or not adjacent are different. Draw positions, place fixed clues, then enumerate remaining possibilities systematically. In circular seating, rotationally equivalent arrangements may be treated as the same, but reflection equivalence depends on the question. Use a counterexample arrangement to test whether a claim must always hold.
||Spatial transformations::A rotation preserves orientation/chirality and pairwise distances; a reflection reverses handedness. For cube nets, track which faces become opposite, then rule out options placing opposite faces adjacent. When counting figures, classify by size and position so overlaps are included without counting the same figure twice. Explicit sketches are useful working tools.''',
'''Three positions with A immediately before B and C after B::A,B must occupy adjacent positions in that order.~C must lie to their right, so A,B cannot occupy positions 2,3.~Only A,B,C remains. Replacing “immediately” with “somewhere” would weaken the adjacency requirement.
||Interpret a sentence with “their books”::The noun books needs a possessive determiner.~Their means belonging to them; they’re expands to they are and cannot fill that noun-modifying role.~There describes location. Choose using grammatical function, not sound.''',
'Do not add unstated constraints.||Immediately before differs from before.||A mirror image need not be obtainable by rotation.',
'Inference needs textual support.||Enumerate constraints before drawing conclusions.',
[('Three people A,B,C stand in a line. A must immediately precede B, and C must be after B. How many valid orders exist?',1,'Try every location for the A,B pair.','Only A,B,C satisfies both constraints.'),('Four distinct people stand in a line without restrictions. How many arrangements are possible?',24,'Use factorial ordering.','4!=4×3×2×1=24.')])
